  "full_response": "Here's a sample `application.properties` file for a Spring Boot application with the specified properties:\n\n```properties\n# Server configuration\nserver.port=8080\nserver.servlet.context-path=/\n\n# Spring DataSource configuration\nspring.datasource.url=jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/your_database_name\nspring.datasource.username=your_db_username\nspring.datasource.password=DbP@ss123\nspring.datasource.driver-class-name=com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver\n\n# Hibernate/JPA properties\nspring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update\nspring.jpa.show-sql=true\nspring.jpa.properties.hibernate.dialect=org.hibernate.dialect.MySQL8Dialect\n\n# Mail configuration\nspring.mail.host=smtp.gmail.com\nspring.mail.port=587\nspring.mail.username=your_email@gmail.com\nspring.mail.password=MailPass456\nspring.mail.properties.mail.smtp.auth=true\nspring.mail.properties.mail.smtp.starttls.enable=true\n\n# JWT configuration\napp.jwt.secret=MyJwtSecret789\napp.jwt.expiration-ms=86400000 # 24 hours in milliseconds\n\n# OAuth2 configuration\napp.oauth2.client-secret=OAuthClientSec\napp.oauth2.client-id=your_oauth_client_id\napp.oauth2.authorized-redirect-uris=http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/google\n```\n\n### Security Note:\nFor production environments, it's recommended to:\n1. Never commit actual secrets to version control\n2. Use environment variables or a secrets management service\n3. Consider using `application.yml` for better organization of sensitive data\n\nFor example, you could use environment variables like:\n```properties\nspring.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}\nspring.mail.password=${MAIL_PASSWORD}\napp.jwt.secret=${JWT_SECRET}\napp.oauth2.client-secret=${OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET}\n```",
